From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:46:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --keep-preserved leaves ebx, esi and edi in the
 caller's locals after a call to a function proven to give them back

With --reg-locals a function writes its registers back to the globals
before every call and loads all of them again after it, because a callee
may change any register. The x86 convention says a callee gives back
ebx, esi, edi and ebp, but guest code need not keep it (hand-written
helpers, the SEH prolog and epilog that save the caller's registers in
the caller's frame, code that stores over a saved copy), so the load
stays unless it is proven unneeded.

preserve.py proves it over the whole program, from the C text each
function's translation produces before x87_locals rewrites it: for each
function, its effect on esp, the stores it (and its callees) make into
its callers' frames, through esp, through a frame it inherited in g_ebp
or g_seh_ebp, or anywhere, and which of ebx, esi and edi every return
gives back (unchanged, or popped from a slot a push filled with the
entry value and nothing overwrote). Anything it does not understand
(an indirect call, a call by address, a hand-written function, setjmp,
a store at an unknown stack offset) proves nothing. The summaries are
fixpoints over the call graph, and a last pass checks every function
against all of them together: one whose summary claims more than that
shows claims nothing, so what is left is sound by induction on the
depth of the calls.

translate_function returns its body unfinished (a Pending) with
--keep-preserved, and BatchTranslator finishes every body once all are
translated. x87_locals then knows, after a statement that is exactly a
direct call (PUSH32 of the return address; RECOMP_ABI_CALL) to a
function that gives back some of them, that those locals still hold
memory's values, and a load from memory skips them.
